-- ScholarMesh -- 08c -- seed notes: Deep Learning & NLP + Algorithms
INSERT INTO DOCUMENT (title, description, file_path, file_type, file_size_bytes, visibility, content, student_id, course_id, upload_date)
SELECT v.title, v.description, v.file_path, 'md', octet_length(v.content), v.visibility, v.content,
       s.student_id, c.course_id, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP - v.age
FROM (VALUES
  ('Transformers and Self-Attention Explained'::varchar, 'Why attention replaced recurrence, how queries, keys and values produce attention weights, and what multi-head attention and positional encodings add.'::varchar, 'seed/transformers-self-attention.md'::varchar, 'public'::varchar, 'sneha.kulkarni@scholarmesh.test'::varchar, 'UE23CS353A'::varchar, INTERVAL '25 days', $n$Recurrent networks read a sentence one token at a time and squeeze everything seen so far into one hidden state. On long sequences that is a bottleneck: early information gets diluted, gradients struggle to travel back over many steps, and training cannot be parallelised because step t needs step t-1. The Transformer (2017) drops recurrence and lets every token look directly at every other token.

In self-attention, each token's embedding is multiplied by three learned matrices to give a query (what am I looking for?), a key (what do I contain?) and a value (what do I pass on if chosen?). The score between tokens i and j is the dot product of query i and key j, divided by sqrt(d_k) so large dimensions do not push the softmax into saturation. Softmax turns each token's scores into weights that sum to 1, and its output is the weighted sum of all value vectors: Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(QK^T / sqrt(d_k)) V.

Take "the animal did not cross the street because it was tired". The query for "it" should put a high weight on "animal". Multi-head attention runs several attention operations in parallel with different projections, so one head can follow coreference while another follows nearby syntax; the outputs are concatenated and projected back.

Attention itself ignores word order, so positional encodings (sinusoids or learned vectors) are added to the embeddings. The original model has an encoder, a stack of self-attention layers that reads the source, and a decoder, which uses masked self-attention (no peeking at future tokens) plus cross-attention to the encoder. BERT keeps only the encoder; GPT keeps only the decoder.$n$::text),
  ('Word Embeddings: word2vec and GloVe', 'One-hot versus dense vectors, the distributional hypothesis, skip-gram and CBOW with negative sampling, GloVe, and cosine similarity.', 'seed/word-embeddings.md', 'public', 'meera.pillai@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS353A', INTERVAL '31 days', $n$A one-hot vector represents each word as a long sparse vector with a single 1, one dimension per vocabulary word. It is huge, and every pair of words is equally far apart, so "cat" is no closer to "kitten" than to "tractor". Dense embeddings fix this by giving each word a short vector, typically 100 to 300 numbers, where similar words end up close together.

The idea comes from the distributional hypothesis: you shall know a word by the company it keeps. Words that appear in similar contexts ("coffee" and "tea" both appear near "drink" and "cup") should get similar vectors. word2vec turns this into a prediction task with a tiny neural network. CBOW predicts the centre word from the surrounding context words; skip-gram does the reverse and predicts each context word from the centre word, which works better for rare words.

A full softmax over the whole vocabulary is too slow, so training uses negative sampling: push the vectors of a real (centre, context) pair together and push a few randomly drawn non-neighbours apart. GloVe takes a different route. It first builds a global word-word co-occurrence matrix, then fits vectors so that the dot product of two word vectors approximates the log of how often they co-occur.

The famous result is the analogy king - man + woman ≈ queen: the offset from "man" to "king" points roughly the same way as the offset from "woman" to "queen". We compare vectors with cosine similarity, a·b / (|a||b|), which ignores length and measures only the angle. Limitation: one vector per word, so "bank" (river) and "bank" (money) are mixed; contextual models like BERT fix this.$n$),
  ('RNNs, LSTMs and the Vanishing Gradient', 'How RNNs unroll through time, why gradients vanish or explode, and how LSTM gates, GRUs and gradient clipping help.', 'seed/rnn-lstm-vanishing-gradient.md', 'public', 'siddharth.rao@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS353A', INTERVAL '19 days', $n$A recurrent neural network (RNN) reads a sequence one step at a time and carries a hidden state forward: h_t = tanh(W_h h_(t-1) + W_x x_t + b). The same weights are reused at every step, so any sequence length works. Training uses backpropagation through time (BPTT): unroll the network across the sequence, treat it as a very deep feed-forward net with shared weights, and add up the gradients from every step.

The trouble is in that unrolled chain. The gradient reaching an early hidden state is a product of one Jacobian per step, each involving W_h and the slope of tanh. If the factors are mostly below 1, the product shrinks exponentially (vanishing gradient) and the network cannot learn dependencies more than about 10 to 20 steps apart. If they are above 1, it blows up (exploding gradient) and the loss becomes NaN. Exploding gradients have a cheap fix, gradient clipping: rescale the gradient whenever its norm exceeds a threshold.

The LSTM fights vanishing gradients with a separate cell state c_t that is only changed by small additive updates, so gradients can flow back through it almost untouched. Three sigmoid gates, each between 0 and 1, control it: forget (how much old cell state to keep), input (how much new information to write) and output (how much of the cell state to expose as h_t). In a language model, for example, the forget gate can clear the stored subject once a sentence ends.

The GRU is a lighter variant with two gates (update and reset) and no separate cell state. It has fewer parameters and often matches an LSTM with less compute.$n$),
  ('Convolutional Neural Networks Basics', 'Filters, feature maps, stride, padding, parameter sharing, pooling and receptive fields, ending with a typical image classifier layout.', 'seed/cnn-basics.md', 'course', 'rohan.gupta@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS353A', INTERVAL '14 days', $n$A convolution layer slides a small filter (kernel), say 3x3, across the image. At each position it takes the dot product of the filter with the patch underneath, giving one number; the grid of these numbers is a feature map. Each filter learns one pattern, such as a vertical edge, and a layer uses many filters.

Stride is how many pixels the filter jumps each step, and padding adds a border of zeros. For input width W, filter size F, padding P and stride S, the output width is floor((W - F + 2P) / S) + 1. A 32x32 image with a 3x3 filter, padding 1 and stride 1 gives 32; with stride 2 it gives 16.

Parameter sharing is the key trick: the same filter weights are used at every location. A 3x3 filter on an RGB image has only 3 x 3 x 3 = 27 weights plus a bias, whereas one fully connected neuron on a 224x224x3 image needs about 150,000. It also detects a pattern wherever it appears in the image.

Max pooling with a 2x2 window and stride 2 keeps the largest value in each window, halving height and width, cutting computation and giving some tolerance to small shifts. The receptive field is the region of the input that can influence one unit; it grows with depth, so early layers see edges, middle layers textures and parts, and deep layers whole objects.

A typical classifier stacks conv, ReLU and pool blocks, then flattens into fully connected layers ending in a softmax. For CIFAR-10 (32x32 colour images, 10 classes): conv 32 filters, pool, conv 64 filters, pool, FC 128, FC 10.$n$),
  ('Backpropagation Without the Mystery', 'A worked two-layer example of the forward pass, the chain rule backward pass and a gradient descent update, plus why activations are cached.', 'seed/backpropagation.md', 'public', 'ananya.rao@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS353A', INTERVAL '4 days', $n$Training a network means nudging its weights to reduce a loss. Backpropagation is simply an efficient way to get the gradient of the loss with respect to every weight, using the chain rule: run a forward pass, compute the loss, then walk backward layer by layer, reusing earlier work.

Take a tiny network with one input: z = w1 x, h = ReLU(z), y = w2 h, loss L = 0.5 (y - t)^2. Let x = 2, t = 1, w1 = 0.5, w2 = 3. Forward: z = 1, h = 1, y = 3, L = 2. Backward, one link at a time: dL/dy = y - t = 2; dL/dw2 = dL/dy * h = 2; dL/dh = dL/dy * w2 = 6; ReLU has slope 1 when z > 0, so dL/dz = 6; finally dL/dw1 = dL/dz * x = 12.

Why cache activations? The gradient for w2 needs h, and the gradient for w1 needs x and z (for the ReLU slope). These come from the forward pass, so we store them instead of recomputing. The price is memory that grows with depth and batch size. The payoff is speed: one backward pass costs about as much as a forward pass, instead of one forward pass per weight.

The update is gradient descent: w = w - lr * dL/dw. With lr = 0.01, w2 becomes 3 - 0.02 = 2.98 and w1 becomes 0.5 - 0.12 = 0.38. A new forward pass gives y = 2.98 * 0.76, about 2.26, and a loss of about 0.80, down from 2. In PyTorch this is loss.backward() then optimizer.step().$n$),
  ('Dynamic Programming Patterns', 'Overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure, memoization versus tabulation, and how to define the state for knapsack and LCS.', 'seed/dynamic-programming-patterns.md', 'public', 'ananya.rao@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS343A', INTERVAL '34 days', $n$Dynamic programming (DP) works when a problem has two properties. Overlapping subproblems: the same smaller problems come up again and again. Optimal substructure: an optimal answer is built from optimal answers to subproblems. The trick is to solve each subproblem once and store the result, turning an exponential recursion into a polynomial one.

Fibonacci is the simplest case. Naive recursion, fib(n) = fib(n-1) + fib(n-2), recomputes the same values over and over and takes exponential time. Memoization (top-down) keeps the recursion but adds a cache, so each fib(k) is computed once. Tabulation (bottom-up) fills an array from the base cases upward. Both are O(n), and tabulation can use O(1) space by keeping only the last two values.

The hard part is defining the state: what is the smallest amount of information that describes a subproblem? For 0/1 knapsack, let dp[i][w] be the best value using the first i items with capacity w. Item i is either skipped or taken: dp[i][w] = max(dp[i-1][w], v_i + dp[i-1][w - w_i]), where taking is allowed only if w_i <= w. There are n x W states with O(1) work each, so the time is O(nW).

For longest common subsequence, let dp[i][j] be the LCS length of the first i characters of A and the first j of B. If A[i] equals B[j], dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1; otherwise dp[i][j] = max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]). For ABCBDAB and BDCABA the answer is 4 (for example BCBA).

My checklist: define the state, write the recurrence, set base cases, pick an evaluation order, and read the answer from the final state.$n$),
  ('Graph Traversals: BFS, DFS and Topological Sort', 'Adjacency lists, BFS for shortest paths, DFS and cycle detection, and topological sorting with Kahn''s algorithm in O(V+E).', 'seed/graph-traversals.md', 'public', 'nikhil.bhat@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS343A', INTERVAL '23 days', $n$A graph is usually stored as an adjacency list: for each vertex, a list of its neighbours. This takes O(V + E) space, unlike an adjacency matrix, which needs O(V^2). Both traversals below visit every vertex once and look at every edge once, so they run in O(V + E).

BFS (breadth-first search) uses a queue. Start from the source; repeatedly dequeue a vertex and enqueue each unvisited neighbour, setting dist[v] = dist[u] + 1. It explores in layers, so the first time BFS reaches a vertex is along a path with the fewest edges. That makes BFS the tool for shortest paths in unweighted graphs; with edge weights you need Dijkstra.

DFS (depth-first search) goes as deep as possible before backtracking, using recursion or an explicit stack. It is good for connected components and cycle detection. In a directed graph, colour vertices white (unseen), grey (on the current recursion path) or black (finished); a cycle exists exactly when DFS meets a grey vertex, which is a back edge. In an undirected graph, a cycle exists if you reach a visited vertex that is not the parent.

A topological sort orders the vertices of a DAG (directed acyclic graph) so that every edge u -> v has u before v, like course prerequisites. Kahn's algorithm: compute all in-degrees, queue the vertices with in-degree 0, then repeatedly pop one, append it to the order and decrement its neighbours' in-degrees, queueing any that reach 0. If fewer than V vertices come out, there is a cycle. For edges A->B, A->C, B->D, C->D the order is A, B, C, D. Time is O(V + E).$n$),
  ('Greedy Algorithms and the Exchange Argument', 'The greedy-choice property, activity selection and Huffman coding, proving correctness by exchange argument, and a coin change counterexample.', 'seed/greedy-exchange-argument.md', 'course', 'diya.shah@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS343A', INTERVAL '16 days', $n$A greedy algorithm builds a solution by taking the best-looking choice at each step and never revisiting it. It is correct only when the problem has the greedy-choice property (some optimal solution contains the greedy choice) and optimal substructure. Otherwise it is fast but wrong.

Activity selection: given intervals, pick the maximum number that do not overlap. Greedy rule: sort by finish time, take the earliest-finishing interval, discard everything that overlaps it, repeat. With A(1,4), B(3,5), C(0,6), D(5,7), E(8,9) we take A, skip B and C, then take D and E: three activities.

The proof technique is the exchange argument. Take any optimal solution O and show you can swap one of its choices for the greedy choice without making it worse, so an optimal solution containing the greedy choice exists; then repeat on what remains, by induction. For activity selection, let g finish earliest and let o be the first interval in O. Since g finishes no later than o, replacing o by g cannot clash with the rest of O, and the count is unchanged.

Huffman coding builds an optimal prefix code. Repeatedly take the two rarest symbols from a min-heap and merge them into one node whose frequency is their sum; the final tree gives the codes, so frequent symbols get short ones. It runs in O(n log n), and the proof is another exchange argument.

Greedy fails on coin change with coins 1, 3, 4 and amount 6. Greedy takes 4, 1, 1: three coins, but 3 + 3 uses two. It works for coin systems like 1, 5, 10, 25, but in general use DP.$n$),
  ('Solving Recurrences with the Master Theorem', 'Solving T(n) = aT(n/b) + f(n) by comparing f(n) with n^(log_b a), the three cases with worked examples, and when the theorem fails.', 'seed/master-theorem.md', 'private', 'siddharth.rao@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS343A', INTERVAL '7 days', $n$The Master Theorem solves divide-and-conquer recurrences of the form T(n) = a T(n/b) + f(n), with a >= 1 and b > 1: the problem splits into a subproblems of size n/b, and f(n) is the cost of dividing and combining. The deciding comparison is f(n) against n^(log_b a), the total work at the leaves of the recursion tree.

Write c = log_b a. There are three cases:
- Case 1: f(n) = O(n^(c - epsilon)) for some epsilon > 0. The leaves dominate, so T(n) = Theta(n^c).
- Case 2: f(n) = Theta(n^c). Every level costs about the same, so T(n) = Theta(n^c log n).
- Case 3: f(n) = Omega(n^(c + epsilon)) and a f(n/b) <= k f(n) for some k < 1. The root dominates, so T(n) = Theta(f(n)).

Examples. Merge sort, T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n: c = 1 and f(n) = n, case 2, so Theta(n log n). Binary search, T(n) = T(n/2) + 1: c = 0 and f(n) = 1 = n^0, case 2, so Theta(log n). Strassen, T(n) = 7T(n/2) + n^2: c = log_2 7, about 2.81, and n^2 is polynomially smaller, case 1, so Theta(n^2.81). For T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n^2, c = 1 and n^2 is polynomially larger, case 3, so Theta(n^2).

When it does not apply: f(n) squeezed between the cases with no polynomial gap (T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n/log n), a that is not a constant, subproblems of unequal size, or recurrences like T(n) = T(n-1) + n. Then use a recursion tree, the substitution method, or Akra-Bazzi.$n$)
) AS v(title, description, file_path, visibility, owner_email, course_code, age, content)
JOIN STUDENT s     ON s.email = v.owner_email
LEFT JOIN COURSE c ON c.course_code = v.course_code;
